  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 332 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n===== Monday - Wednesday =====\n\nSfc low pressure will be sitting across the area on Monday, which \nwill support at least some isolated to scattered showers and storms \nacross the area. In addition to those chances, Monday features quite \na tricky temperature forecast, with a north-south gradient expected \non where that sfc low sits. There is potential for us to see temps \nin the upper 70s near Madison IN, to lower 90s across south-central \nKY Monday afternoon. Any severe potential will be limited to the \nsouth side of the low where better heating will be, so overall \nsevere risk appears low at this time. \n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday, we'll be in a post-frontal cooler airmass \nwith an upper trough overhead, which will bring a welcoming change \nin temps to the region. Highs on Tuesday still could be split north \nto south, with mid-70s north of the WK/BG Pkwys, and mid--80s to the \nsouth. However, by Wednesday, we begin to see more fall-like temps \nwith highs in the 70s and overnight lows in the 50s. We'll still \nhave some precip chances during this time due to broad lift from the \ntrough, but a gradual exit to the east is expect by Wednesday night. \n\n===== Late Next Week =====\n\nThe upper pattern will amplify over late next week with the upper \ntrough deepening into an upper low over the East Coast, and ridging \nbuilding across the Central Plains. This will place the Ohio Valley \nin a more persistent NW flow, which will result in amplifying our \ncooler pattern. The result could be more days with highs in the 70s \nand overnight lows in the 50s. Dry weather is expected through the \nend of the week with sfc high pressure moving across the Great Lakes \nregion.",
